 "subject": "SE of shape and scale parameter estimates in R",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2020-03-26T12:59:57Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "AfM5nZ9uBQAJ",
   "from": "Elaine Gallenberg",
   "author": "8c434d4aad54fb95",
   "date": "2020-03-26T04:55:18Z",
   "subject": "SE of shape and scale parameter estimates in R",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello, <div><br></div><div>I have a question about the standard error for the parameters when estimating a detection function in R. I know when using Distance in R, the parameter estimates are given on the log scale. For my own analysis with a hazard rate key function (no adjustment terms), I would like to report report the estimates and standard errors of sigma and b (not log(sigma) and log(b) ). My question is, what is the SE column in the ddf information reporting? Is it SE(log(sigma)) and SE(log(b))? If so, how to I obtain the standard error of sigma and b? For reference, this is the output from my model: </div><div><br></div><div><div>Detection function parameters </div><div>Scale coefficient(s):  </div><div>            estimate        se</div><div>(Intercept) 2.330206 0.2733554</div><div><br></div><div>Shape coefficient(s):  </div><div>            estimate        se</div><div>(Intercept) 0.677233 0.2835995</div></div><div><br></div><div>Thank you much, </div><div>Elaine </div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "_c33Y1eRBgAJ",
   "from": "Eric Rexstad",
   "author": "f2122b34f1aa6e95",
   "date": "2020-03-26T08:22:07Z",
   "subject": "{Disarmed} Re: [distance-sampling] SE of shape and scale parameter estimates in R",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>Elaine</div><br><div>You are correct that the reported SE is SE of log(parameter estimate).</div><br><div>To find the SE on the real scale, make use of the delta method approximation.  Consult Powell (2007) if you&#39;ve not met the delta method before.</div><br><div>The delta method works with variances, rather than SEs.  You&#39;ll need to convert your SEs to variances, perform the delta method calculation, then square root to produce SE of the transformed variance.</div><br><div>My math is probably dubious this morning, so best to check, but I think this is a possible answer</div><br><div><font style=\"font-family:monospace\"><a href=\"http://new.se\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://new.se&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792473500306000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2Y4HS4ifGeNmH1XlTMsqQD\">new.se</a> &lt;- sqrt(exp(se)^2 * exp(se)^2)</font></div><br><div><div>Powell, Larkin A. 2007. Approximating variance of demographic parameters using the delta method: a reference for avian biologists. <em>The Condor</em> 109 (4): 949–54. <a href=\"https://link.getmailspring.com/link/BA1...@getmailspring.com/0?redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.1650%2F0010-5422(2007)109%5B949%3AAVODPU%5D2.0.CO%3B2&amp;recipient=ZGlzdGFuY2Utc2FtcGxpbmdAZ29vZ2xlZ3JvdXBzLmNvbQ%3D%3D\" title=\"https://doi.org/10.1650/0010-5422(2007)109[949:AVODPU]2.0.CO;2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://link.getmailspring.com/link/BA1...@getmailspring.com/0?redirect%3Dhttps%253A%252F%252Fdoi.org%252F10.1650%252F0010-5422(2007)109%255B949%253AAVODPU%255D2.0.CO%253B2%26recipient%3DZGlzdGFuY2Utc2FtcGxpbmdAZ29vZ2xlZ3JvdXBzLmNvbQ%253D%253D&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792473500306000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1FsyVaqLNe3LjaN5b2KVe6\"><font color=\"red\"><b>MailScanner has detected a possible fraud attempt from &quot;link.getmailspring.com&quot; claiming to be</b></font> https://doi.org/10.1650/0010-5422(2007)109[949:AVODPU]2.0.CO;2</a>.</div></div><br><div><u></u><table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tbody><tr><td style=\"vertical-align:top;width:1px\"></td><td><div><strong>Eric Rexstad</strong></div><div>Senior Research Fellow, CREEM, Univ. of St Andrews, Charity SC013532</div></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><div style=\"font-size:0.9em;border-top:1px dashed gray;min-width:250px;max-width:400px;margin-top:10px;padding-top:4px\"><div><a style=\"margin-right:8px\" href=\"https://link.getmailspring.com/link/BA1...@getmailspring.com/1?redirect=mai...%40st-andrews.ac.uk&amp;recipient=ZGlzdGFuY2Utc2FtcGxpbmdAZ29vZ2xlZ3JvdXBzLmNvbQ%3D%3D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://link.getmailspring.com/link/BA1...@getmailspring.com/1?redirect%3Dmailto%253Aeric.rexstad%2540st-andrews.ac.uk%26recipient%3DZGlzdGFuY2Utc2FtcGxpbmdAZ29vZ2xlZ3JvdXBzLmNvbQ%253D%253D&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792473500306000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0bjgX5HJ_HNM0VyOIrTQX2\">eric.r...@st-andrews.ac.uk</a><a style=\"margin-right:8px\" href=\"tel:+44%20(0)1334%20461833\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">+44 (0)1334 461833</a></div><div><a style=\"margin-right:8px\" href=\"https://link.getmailspring.com/link/BA1...@getmailspring.com/2?redirect=http%3A%2F%2Ferex.github.io&amp;recipient=ZGlzdGFuY2Utc2FtcGxpbmdAZ29vZ2xlZ3JvdXBzLmNvbQ%3D%3D\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://link.getmailspring.com/link/BA1...@getmailspring.com/2?redirect%3Dhttp%253A%252F%252Ferex.github.io%26recipient%3DZGlzdGFuY2Utc2FtcGxpbmdAZ29vZ2xlZ3JvdXBzLmNvbQ%253D%253D&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792473500306000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3GdKMiyAC6xQ5krQdSgWBJ\"><font color=\"red\"><b>MailScanner has detected a possible fraud attempt from &quot;link.getmailspring.com&quot; claiming to be</b></font> http://erex.github.io</a></div><div><a href=\"https://link.getmailspring.com/link/BA1...@getmailspri